WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.923
- Welcome back.

00:00:00.923 --> 00:00:02.190
I'm today's guest host Howie Mandel.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.815
Here with Terry Crews and my
daughter Jacqueline Schulz.

00:00:04.815 --> 00:00:06.060
(audience applauds)

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.220
Pretty much all in the family this hour.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.440
Now, if you ever wished you knew more

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.380
about your family's history,
our next guest can help.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.440
She's an author from Louisiana
who captures memories

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.560
and turns them into actual written books.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.490
She's what our season-long
partner Pilot Pen, makers of G2,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.150
the go-to pen of "Kelly Clarkson
Show" calls unstoppable.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.490
That's because she's writing history.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.861
We have Olivia dialed in.

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:32.951
(audience applauds)

00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:34.110
- Yeah.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.291
- How are you?

00:00:35.291 --> 00:00:36.124
- I'm doing great.

00:00:36.124 --> 00:00:37.500
Thanks so much for having me.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.820
- Oh, it's my pleasure.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.710
It's the least I could do.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.110
Anyway, you are a biographer.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.580
This is, I find this fascinating

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.590
'cause everybody loves reading biographies

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.560
but what would be more important
than reading a biography

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.810
of somebody you care
about, you love, you know

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.250
you wanna know more about?

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.350
So explain how it works.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.830
- Sure, so I write life story
books for everyday people.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.390
For grandparents.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.330
I write books for people,
usually 65 and older.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.130
They're in their seventies, their eighties

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.840
or even their nineties.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.970
And sometimes they call
me up to commission

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.970
me to write this life story
book for their family.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.000
And other times it's
been their adult child

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.610
or their grandchild
commissioning me so that

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.450
the whole family can enjoy
their loved one's story forever.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.660
The way I do this is based
off of verbal interviews.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.120
I ask guided questions starting

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.320
with their earliest memories.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.840
We go through their teen
years, their love stories

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.380
a whole lifetime of memories.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.213
- [Terry] Wow.

00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:34.620
- And I have personally written

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.930
over 40 of these life story books.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.090
- Wow, and what inspired you to do this?

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:39.923
Oh, give her a hand.

00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:40.756
(audience applauds)
- Yes.

00:01:40.756 --> 00:01:43.200
40.
40 biographies.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.700
What inspired, how did you get inspired?

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.690
What started you?

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.270
- My grandmothers inspired me to do this.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.630
I grew up in South Louisiana,
where they are from

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.190
and where we all live.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.900
And I grew up close to them.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.530
I am still so close to them
and I was so surprised to learn

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.390
that I didn't know them quite as well

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:04.440
as I thought until I worked
on these life story books.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.440
So I learned so much from
interviewing my grandmothers.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.440
And after seeing what those
books meant to my own family,

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.510
I knew I wanted to do this for others.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.768
- I have a grandmother who used to.

00:02:13.768 --> 00:02:15.480
(audience applauds)

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.960
My grandmother used to steal
Yo-Yos from the neighbors

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.100
and give them to us.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.855
I wish we had a place to write that down.

00:02:21.855 --> 00:02:22.688
(Terry laughs)

00:02:22.688 --> 00:02:26.220
Terry, do you have any relatives that

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.098
you think would be interesting?

00:02:27.098 --> 00:02:28.290
- Oh absolutely.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.050
I have a 95 year old grandmother.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.349
- I met her at your star
on The Walk of Fame.

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:35.130
- The Walk of Fame, you met her.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.360
- That's an amazing story.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.350
- It's an amazing story.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.270
I would love to get in contact with you

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.700
because she told me, I was writing my book

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.228
and she told me so many
stories that I didn't know

00:02:44.228 --> 00:02:45.450
about my family.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.470
- She's lived through so much.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.910
- I mean, and this is
something you can pass

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.950
down to your kids and their kids.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.450
It's something that could go on forever.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.040
- These aren't short books, right?

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.360
These aren't pamphlets?

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.390
- No, these are full-length
life story books.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.060
So here's one of my grandmother's books.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.210
This one has 100 pages in it, for example.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.170
And here's one a for

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.350
a World War II veteran
and lifelong educator.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.170
And this one is closer to 200 pages.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.270
So everyone is so different.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.420
They are based off of verbal interviews

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.030
and after about 12 or 15 hours,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.790
in small increments, of
talking with someone,

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.760
either remotely or in the
comfort of their home,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.660
I write for 6 to 12 weeks usually.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.200
And then I help them pick
their most cherished photos.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.260
One of my favorite things
to do is to have them use

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.800
a pen and hand write
Chapter One, Chapter Two

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.650
and sign off their books in the end.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.667
- I got a question, do you
ever have a spouse that says,

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:44.484
"Nah, that didn't happen."

00:03:44.484 --> 00:03:45.317
(Olivia laughs)

00:03:45.317 --> 00:03:46.150
You know what I mean?

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:47.190
Like, I can see my wife being

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.870
like, "No, that's not how it went at all."

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:49.985
- I'm not lying.
It's in a book.

00:03:49.985 --> 00:03:52.560
(all laugh)

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.110
- Well, actually, not
too much because I work,

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.027
usually one-on-one with a subject,

00:03:57.027 --> 00:04:00.840
and it's their book, it's their
story the way they tell it.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.400
So that hasn't come up.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.540
- Well I'm gonna call you.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:04.950
I don't wanna do a whole
book 'cause I don't

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.260
have attention span, but I'd
love a pamphlet for the kids.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.160
Of me.
(all laugh)

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.563
Anyways. thank you so much, Olivia.

